            <ArticleTitle>Gelatinous bone marrow transformation secondary to unusual eating habits and drastic weight loss.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2013-200243</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr2013200243</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Gelatinous bone marrow transformation (GMT), also known as starvation bone marrow, has been reported in a number of chronic illnesses, eating disorders (anorexia nervosa) and malignancies. We report the case of a 37-year-old man with a history of bipolar disorder and obesity (weighing &gt;300 pounds) who presented due to recently developing a deep yellow colour to his skin. Over the past 2 years, through diet and exercise, he lost over 150 pounds. He reported running 6-8 miles per day and eating 'lots of squash'. We made the diagnosis of starvation hepatitis and bone marrow degeneration, and referred the patient to a dietician and haematologist/oncologist, where improvements were observed at 4 weeks follow-up.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
